Pterygium
Opthalmology & ENT
Fleshy triangular fibrovascular growth, Corneal invasion, Nasal conjunctival location

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-037
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of chronic ocular irritation resulting in severe foreign body sensation, intense redness, and functional visual impairment due to tearing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with a rapidly worsening, fleshy growth on the eye, now presenting with severe, acute localized redness, tearing, and blurring of vision. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to differentiate an inflamed, encroaching pterygium from a sight-threatening infectious marginal keratitis or an aggressively enlarging ocular surface squamous neoplasia (OSSN), ensuring the central visual axis was not in immediate jeopardy before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The fleshy web on my eye has gone completely bloodshot, it feels like I have sandpaper stuck under my lid, and my vision is smudged."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a known history of a fleshy, triangular, fibrovascular tissue growth originating from the nasal bulbar conjunctiva. Over the last 48 hours, they experienced an episodic, symptomatic inflammatory flare-up presenting with intensely increased swelling and redness. Endorses severe chronic foreign body sensation, burning, and persistent grittiness. Notes progressive, mild visual impairment described as smudged or stretched vision, secondary to induced astigmatism and localized tearing. Clear history of chronic, cumulative environmental exposure to sun and surf (avid surfer).
Functional Status: Highly irritated, keeping the affected eye partially closed.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, frustrated by the continuous ocular irritation, utilizing tissues to manage reflex tearing.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity is 6/9 in the affected eye, clearing to 6/6 with pinhole, confirming induced astigmatism rather than central axis obscuration. Examination reveals a fleshy, triangular, fibrovascular growth extending from the nasal conjunctiva directly onto the medial cornea by 2mm. The lesion is intensely hyperemic with localized conjunctival injection. A faint Stocker's line (iron deposition) is visible at the advancing leading edge. The central cornea remains perfectly clear, and the pupil is briskly reactive. No purulent discharge.
Environmental Observations: Surfboards and wetsuits visible in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acutely Inflamed Pterygium - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ocular Surface Squamous Neoplasia (OSSN) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The classic triangular, flat, fibrovascular appearance of the lesion, lacking the leukoplakic, gelatinous, or highly elevated, irregular, and friable characteristics of carcinoma in situ.

Marginal Keratitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of a sterile white corneal infiltrate separated from the limbus by a clear zone; the pathology here is a continuous fibrovascular overgrowth from the conjunctiva.

Pinguecula - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The definitive, visible invasion of the fibrovascular tissue crossing over the limbus directly onto the clear cornea, which definitively differentiates it from a strictly conjunctival pinguecula.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, acute inflammatory flare of a pre-existing pterygium, causing functional distress due to intense foreign body sensation and induced reflex tearing. Urgent bedside evaluation was critical to verify the exact extent of the corneal invasion, ensuring the central visual axis was not immediately threatened and ruling out infectious keratitis at the lesion's leading edge. By definitively diagnosing an inflammatory flare of a benign (though invasive) fibrovascular lesion, the urgent visit prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission, provided targeted, heavy topical anti-inflammatory therapy, and allowed for a structured, elective surgical referral pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical NSAID drops (Ketorolac 0.5%) QID for 5 days to rapidly suppress the acute fibrovascular inflammation. Prescribed intensive, preservative-free lubricating drops hourly to manage the secondary localized dry eye. Generated an urgent referral to an ophthalmologist for definitive surgical excision (pterygiectomy with conjunctival autograft).
Positive Behavioral Change: To drastically reduce the environmental trigger causing the flare, the patient must place their wrap-around, UV-polarized sunglasses physically on top of their car keys and surfboard wax. This forced physical interaction guarantees the protective eyewear is worn during all outdoor exposures (negative reinforcement of the pain by preventing the UV/wind trigger), structurally embedding the essential protective habit required to slow the lesion's growth.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to ensure the acute inflammation has resolved and to track the ophthalmology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the central vision suddenly drops, if a white spot appears on the clear part of the cornea, or if severe, deep aching eye pain develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; definitive management requires specialist surgical excision to prevent permanent astigmatism.
Clinical Course: The acute redness, swelling, and severe scratchiness will improve over the next 3-5 days with the prescribed drops. The fleshy growth itself will not shrink and must be surgically removed.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your vision suddenly worsens significantly, or if the pain changes to a deep, severe ache, you must go to the emergency department.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Chronic sun and wind exposure have caused a fleshy scar tissue to grow from the white of your eye onto the clear window (cornea). The current severe redness is an acute inflammatory flare. The drops will calm the redness, but the growth requires surgery before it permanently distorts your vision.
Actionable Steps: Use the anti-inflammatory drops 4 times a day. Flood the eye with the preservative-free lubricants every hour. You must wear UV-blocking, wrap-around sunglasses at all times when outdoors.

